{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1487793999,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyMDk1NjU2Mjc=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1331","origin":"github"},"title":"RFC:  --no-fast for publish ?","message":"and make `--fast` a default option for `git annex copy` operation of publish\n\n- ATM `publish` is semi-fast already by default \n   -   with an analysis to decide either it even should try to push/sync:\n       ```\n       [DEBUG  ] Testing matching branch to 'master' at 'rolando' for differences \n       [DEBUG  ] No changes detected with respect to state of 'rolando' \n       ```\n       it manages to skip doing anything if it looks like there is nothing to do.  \n       I do not think any option is there to prevent this behavior if e.g. I have changed some config which would have resulted in new actions to be taken if published tried to publish again.\n   - with  default `--since` handling, we manage to avoid even descending into datasets which we probably don't need to (re)publish, which makes `publish` usable in case of any respectfully sized collection.  There is a way to avoid such smartness by providing `--since=`\n- in most IMHO common scenarios of (re)publishing a hierarchy of datasets we need 'fast' mode of operation, so I would lean to keep 'smart fast' behavior a default\n\nSo, for now, we either just need one additional option to overcome smartness of \"changes detection\" (e.g. `--no-changes-detection`) or overall `--no-fast` which would skip all smartness analyses (--since, \"changes detection\" etc) and just would carry all the steps.\n\nWhat do you think?","files":null}]}